onent checked to me on the River, I have two main options:\n1.  **Check:** Showdown immediately. This is the safest play. If my pair of 8s is good, I win. If I am beaten (by a 9, A, or better pair), I lose nothing more. There is little value in betting because worse hands (like high cards) will likely fold, and better hands will call.\n2.  **Bet:** This turns my hand into a bluff or a thin value bet. Given the passive history, a bet might force a fold from high cards, but my hand already beats those. It is unlikely to get called by worse.\n\nChecking maximizes the probability of realizing my equity without risking more chips on a marginal hand.\n\nReason:\nI have a pair of Eights (Third pair on the board). The action has been checked down to the river. Betting provides little value as worse hands will fold and better hands will call. Checking back ensures I get to showdown cheaply to see if my pair is good.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
