air (Queens and Tens) on a board of 10h, 2h, Qd, 9d. You also have a flush draw (Diamonds). This is a very strong hand. The board is "wet," meaning there are several draws available for the opponent (Straight draws like J-K or J-8, and flush draws). The opponent merely called the flop bet, suggesting they might have a made hand like Top Pair or are chasing a draw.\n\nReason: You are currently ahead of most of the opponent\'s range. Checking is risky because it gives the opponent a "free card" that could complete their straight or flush, causing you to lose a big pot. Betting extracts value from worse hands (like a weaker Queen or Ten) and forces opponents on a draw to pay to see the next card. A bet of 400 is roughly two-thirds of the estimated pot (assuming a pot of ~600), which is a standard size to protect your hand and build value.\n\nAnswer: 400'}
