ld?**\n\nFolding is safe but would also be a bit weak given the small bet, but I could get exploited. My pair of fours might be the best, considering this passive play. Calling is cheap and allows me to see the river.\n\n**Final Decision:**\n\nI'm leaning towards calling here. I think it's a good spot to try to catch a card on the river or find out whether I am beat. Since this is an unexploitable scenario and it's a small bet, I am going to call.\n0 seems to be the right action!\n\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: You hold a pocket pair of 4s and a gutshot straight draw (needing a 5 to complete the 2-3-4-5-6 sequence). The opponent played passively pre-flop and on the flop, only betting on the turn. While your pair is vulnerable to any Jack, 6, 3, or higher pocket pair, the bet is relatively small compared to your stack and the pot. You have approximately 6 outs (four 5s for a straight, two 4s for a set) to improve, giving you about 13% equity on the draw alone. Combining this with the chance your opponent is bluffing with overcards, calling is a mathematically defensible play.\n\nReason: The cost to call (107) is low relative to the potential payout and your remaining stack. The combined equity of your current pair (bluff-catching) and your straight draw justifies matching the small bet to see the River card.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
